{"title": "CSCI-569: Seminar in Theory Orientation", "learningObjectives": [
        "Define the stable process for piloting new undergraduate theory courses by identifying faculty-led proposal requirements and cluster classifications.",
        "Explain the standards and planning methods for determining course details, including prerequisites, format, learning outcomes, assessment methods, and",
        "Evaluate representative examples of Theory cluster courses to understand how to apply evaluation criteria for faculty proposals."
      ], "beats": [
        {
          "title": "Course Purpose and Faculty Role",
          "claim": "This course examines current topics in Theory and is intended to allow faculty to pilot potential new undergraduate offerings.",
          "example": "A professor proposes a new undergraduate course in theoretical computer science to test its viability before full adoption.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a slide with the course title and a brief description of the faculty proposal process.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Course Classification Clusters",
          "claim": "Specific course instances will be identified as belonging to the Theory cluster, the Security cluster, or both clusters.",
          "example": "A proposed course on cryptographic protocols is classified under the Security cluster, while a course on computational complexity falls under the Theory cluster.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a Venn diagram with two overlapping circles labeled 'Theory Cluster' and 'Security Cluster', with a third circle labeled 'Both Clusters' in the intersection.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Faculty-Determined Details",
          "claim": "Specific course details, such as prerequisites, course seminar, format, learning outcomes, assessment methods, and resource needs, will be determined by the faculty member(s) who","example": "The proposing faculty member sets the prerequisite to include discrete mathematics and defines the format as a three-hour weekly lecture.",
          "visualDirection": "List the specific course details (prerequisites, seminar, format, learning outcomes, assessment methods, resource needs) with checkboxes for faculty to fill in.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Stable Process Overview",
          "claim": "The stable process for piloting new undergraduate courses involves faculty proposal, cluster identification, and detailed planning.",
          "example": "The department follows a standardized workflow: faculty submit a proposal, assign cluster(s), define details, and pilot the course.",
          "visualDirection": "Create a linear flowchart with four steps: Proposal Submission, Cluster Assignment, Detail Planning, and Course Pilot.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Standards for Course Design",
          "claim": "Course design standards require clear learning outcomes and assessment methods tailored to the proposed topic.",
          "example": "The learning outcomes specify that students will analyze algorithmic correctness and resource bounds, assessed through a final theoretical proof assignment.",
          "visualDirection": "Display a table with columns for 'Learning Outcome' and 'Assessment Method', filled with example entries.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Planning Methods for Resources",
          "claim": "Resource needs must be identified and documented as part of the course planning process.",
          "example": "The faculty member lists required resources, including a whiteboard, projection system, and specific textbooks on theoretical computer science.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a checklist graphic with items like 'Whiteboard', 'Projection System', 'Textbooks', and 'Online Resources' for faculty to mark.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Evaluation Criteria for Proposals",
          "claim": "Evaluation criteria for faculty proposals must assess alignment with cluster standards and feasibility of the pilot course.",
          "example": "The proposal is evaluated based on whether it meets Theory cluster standards and whether the proposed resources are sufficient for a successful pilot.",
          "visualDirection": "Create a rubric with criteria: 'Cluster Alignment', 'Feasibility', 'Learning Outcomes', and 'Resource Availability', with scoring scales.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Representative Examples of Theory Courses",
          "claim": "Representative examples of Theory cluster courses include topics like algorithm analysis, data structures, and formal languages.",
          "example": "A sample Theory cluster course covers loop invariants, divide-and-conquer algorithms, and graph reachability.",
          "visualDirection": "List three example course titles with brief descriptions: 'Introduction to Algorithm Analysis', 'Data Structures and Algorithms', 'Formal Languages and Automata'.","sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Application to Course Planning",
          "claim": "Faculty must apply the stable process and evaluation criteria to finalize their course proposal before pilot implementation.",
          "example": "The faculty member reviews the proposal against the evaluation criteria, makes necessary adjustments, and submits the final plan for departmental approval.",
          "visualDirection": "Show a before-and-after comparison of a course proposal document, highlighting changes made based on evaluation criteria.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        },
        {
          "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
          "claim": "A practical analysis checklist ensures all course details, cluster classifications, and evaluation criteria are met before the pilot begins.",
          "example": "The checklist includes items like 'Proposal submitted', 'Cluster assigned', 'Details defined', 'Resources secured', 'Evaluation criteria met', and 'Pilot approved'.","visualDirection": "Display a printable checklist graphic with checkboxes for each item, ready for faculty to use during the planning phase.",
          "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-569"]
        }
      ]
    }
